  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "While the study links physical LCZ changes to human thermal comfort, the survey findings are based on a single metropolitan area, which may limit direct generalization to cities with different socio-economic or climatic contexts.",
      "what": "This study mapped 16 Local Climate Zone (LCZ) types in the Kolkata Metropolitan Area from 2010 to 2023, analyzing their morphological changes alongside land surface temperatures and resident survey responses. The analysis revealed a significant shift toward compact mid-rise and low-rise built forms at the expense of vegetated areas, with the highest land surface temperatures concentrated in lightweight low-rise and heavy industrial zones. Survey data showed that human heat discomfort is driven by both the intensity of the temperature and the duration of exposure across different urban forms.",
      "why": "It connects physical urban morphology changes directly to human thermal experiences and duration of exposure, moving beyond purely physical temperature measurements to show how specific dense built forms exacerbate daily heat vulnerability."
    }
